75642,"relating":0.05945860464456317,"human":0.03391779703623105,"humans":0.05945860464456317,"structure":0.03660087442206108,"things":0.026320960375127704,"within":0.02954973328921807,"organization":0.052478534747578964,"notable":0.052478534747578964,"quotes":0.052478534747578964,"08":0.04934389788897093,"00":0.038195335992914956,"now":0.015941629133620915,"because":0.01894098301587107,"context":0.030430543955634184,"window":0.04934389788897093,"five":0.06865356690363823,"past":0.04934389788897093,"messages":0.03276562755668558,"here":0.01652640660711314,"entry":0.042972386155516004,"includes":0.048029739533312125,"trail":0.10495706949515793,"mileage":0.05945860464456317,"elevation":0.05945860464456317,"gain":0.05440125126676705,"rough":0.05081300194086629,"estimate":0.04575564856307017,"long":0.0400239164566207,"ll":0.0610490555008089,"take":0.030430543955634184,"plus":0.03564094180747794,"much":0.02770827417888946,"shade":0.05945860464456317,"then":0.016932067341266732,"real":0.03474963642597393,"quick":0.03877557866608596,"rename":0.056675342237009006,"something":0.02119087811403249,"air":0.05440125126676705,"quality":0.046840938696611836,"update":0.03660087442206108,"description":0.043832932043882084,"doing":0.030129975962389078,"questions":0.0361116757162321,"answers":0.04141123026038246,"source":0.15510231466434385,"teach":0.17533172817552833,"just":0.015024085576475193,"first":0.022092931422221476,"25min":0.06304685397046395,"apply":0.043832932043882084,"today":0.035187329340185196}}]},{"id":"doc_1787410477234_6zcjnj","name":"DIGEST_langgraph_complete_course","text":"DIGEST · NEXUS · langgraph_complete_course · runtime ≈3:30:19 · mode:estimate\nTITLE: Building Conversational Workflows with Langraph\nTOPICS: ai · agents · backend · engineering · design\nTHESIS: The course introduces Langraph’s core concepts—type annotations, state, nodes, edges, tools, and runnables—and walks you through building a simple hello‑world graph, giving you the foundation to create robust, scalable conversational AI applications. By mastering these building blocks you can confidently design more complex agent workflows.\n\nCHAPTER 1 — Course introduction and goals\n  [1:51] The reason I've kept this specific section in the course is because when we do eventually go on to code uh our AI agents, our graphs and langraph, these will start popping up everywhere.\n  [4:48] So now there are two main uh benefits of using a type dictionary it's type safety because we've explicitly defined what should be in this data structure and so this will really reduce the runtime errors and obviously the readability is enhanced as well and th…\n  [15:12] Now it's important to note that it doesn't perform any operations itself but it serves as the designated starting position for the graph's execution.\n  [18:15] It manages the nodes, the edges, the overall state and it makes sure that the workflow operates in a unified way and all of the data flows correctly between components.\n\nCHAPTER 2 — Type annotations overview\n  [21:32] because I thought that one we haven't really even seen uh how to actually code in Langraph and combining all of these LLMs APIs and tools and all of that stuff which comes with it combining them together would be quite messy and it could be quite confusing at…\n  [26:01] Now obviously the data type of message will be string right so that's why we specify strl again this is just normal python so once we've done that we are now going to be coding our very first node again another very fundamental element in langraph so how do w…\n  [27:45] So now we need to do something very very important and it gets annoying sometimes but um it's really a key habit which I want you to form and it is dog strings.\n  [35:21] So we simply just put message and perfect you we get the final answer which is hey Bob how's your day going now why is it like this because this is exactly how we set our act how we set our function to be what action it performs it says hey then concatenates…\n\nCHAPTER 3 — Typed dictionaries for safety\n  [42:10] We pass in state result cuz that's what we are uh the action we're performing is on result uh the attribute result and let's say something like hi there and then we refer to the name um cool and your sum is equal to and let's just use the inbuilt Python funct…\n  [44:24] Now here's another important part which is quite a common mistake especially like I have done this many times.\n  [48:59] So for example your input should could be jack sparrow your values 1 2 3 4 again and then your operation uh uh multiplication and your output should be in the format of hi jack sparrow your answer is 24 so just to give you a hint as to how you would perform s…\n  [58:08] So yes you one important thing which you've learned is obviously how to use the add edge method but another concept which you have solidified here is you can uh change these at these keys of your state at in at any point in time like as long as as however man…\n\nCHAPTER 4 — Union, optional, and any types\n  [1:00:26] You are 31 years old and you have skills in Python, machine learning and langraph.\n  [1:02:28] Again, if you remember a few subsections ago, I told you there are multiple ways to be able to initialize the start and the end point.\n  [1:02:53] like standard procedure we will design we will um code up the uh the schema the state schema so class agent state and let's again type dictionary in this case uh uh I want to be able to pass in two numbers and pass in an operation so a plus operation and a mi…\n  [1:19:45] Now, I know this will be quite confusing at first and don't worry, it took me quite a while to understand this myself as well, but hopefully the exercise I've given you will really be able to help you understand this much better.\n\nCHAPTER 5 — Lambda functions for concise logic\n  [1:23:46] The reason is once you finish this course and actually start either making your own AI agentic systems for someone else, for your clients or for yourself like make your own JavaS system or whatever.\n  [1:24:14] where's the start point going to go, end point going to go etc etc and you can either do that via pen and paper or software like I've used but point is you need some sort of blueprint and that's how really it works in the industry as well um you can you will…\n  [1:37:44] So that's really the way which I personally use to create loops in langraph it's pretty easy right but um obviously with practice you might even find some other ways if you do find other ways like obviously uh do let me know uh there's more than one way again…\n  [1:38:42] So for context, you need to set the bounce which we can guess between 1 to 20 integers of course and the graph has to keep guessing where the max number of guesses is 7 where if the guess is correct it stops but if not we keep looping until we hit the max lim…\n\nCHAPTER 6 — Core graph elements: state, node, edge\n  [1:43:04] Okay, so we've already imported type dictionary and list many times before but um these two we haven't sorry these two the lang chain codon messages import human message so I briefly mentioned this in the intro of this section of the subsection what a human m…\n  [1:43:56] Now you must know that langraph is built on top of lang chain and lang chain already has the sophisticated libraries right so why not actually use them that's how langraph is designed it's designed to use the robust sophisticated libraries which lang chain of…\n  [1:44:30] Essentially, it's just a file used to store secret stuff like API keys or configuration values.\n  [1:54:12] We're going to particularly use the GPD4 model again using the lang chains uh chat open AI library and overall we're going to create a sophisticated conversation loop.\n\nCHAPTER 7 — Graph structure and conditional edges\n  [2:01:38] uh it only exits unless if the user has uh inputed well exit obviously but now look at this the conversation history is appended with the human message and the human message is obviously the user input the reason I've kept on writing content is because well t…\n  [2:10:11] the state is locked in within the graph now and uh the conversation history is just another I guess you can say a duplicate version of the state right because we've you we've appended the exact same human messages and AI messages and kept on updating it throu…\n  [2:13:44] But using so many tokens uh using so many tokens like input tokens especially will really eat away uh your uh the amount of money you will uh spend like it will drastically increase it and that's a huge problem right we want to try to be conservative a bit ab…\n  [2:15:50] The technical term for this type of agent is called a react agent and react stands for reasoning and acting.\n\nCHAPTER 8 — Tools and tool nodes explained\n  [2:24:40] So a reducer function is essentially just a rule that controls how updates from nodes are combined with the existing state.\n  [2:25:37] Now in the previous uh graphs and agents we've made we've appended it but now that we're using so many different messages and calls and tool calling and what whatever we can't really always append everything like it will get far too complicated.\n  [2:26:42] Okay, I've cleared the screen now and let's actually begin like we how we always begin with the uh creation of our state of our agent.\n  [2:28:58] Again, I'm using GPD40 because I've never had a problem with it to be honest.\n\nCHAPTER 9 — State graph and runnable components\n  [2:43:26] So it's it's so robust even if we don't need to use a tool it will still give us an answer and the reason it was able to do that is once all the tool calling is done it passes it to the agent the agent checks again oh I need to tell it I need to tell the user…\n  [2:47:44] Now this global variable yes it's a bit odd defining global variables and there's a reason which I've done it and this will become more apparent as I go through the code but just as a heads up the reason I've defined a global variable in this case is to actua…\n  [2:52:22] So this piece of code is just uh some code which allows you to save the uh contents a the the content store in in the global variable under the file name uh and as a text file and I've also added this exception uh which is a good thing for debugging purposes…\n  [2:53:54] And okay so now this doc uh not doc string this we need to pass in a system message to our llm right now this llm this system prompt will be quite large so get ready uh like such so in this system prompt I have specifically said this is a system message and t…\n\nCHAPTER 10 — Message types in Langraph\n  [3:03:33] If we wanted to use more complicated uh form uh complicated uh stuff from langraph like the injected state or even using something like commands and interrupts uh we would have to write the code slightly differently but because this is a beginner level course…\n  [3:08:41] uh using langraph we can really make the agents quite robust and that's the thing which I wanted to show you it doesn't always have to pick a tool its own like LLM like the agent itself cuz remember the agent node has an LLM in the background back end the bin…\n  [3:10:09] So, maybe you could add use OpenAI whisper for uh speech to text conversion or add 11 laps for text to speech conversion and maybe you can make it voice based cuz right now I'm giving it I'm how am I communicating it with text mode?\n  [3:16:40] The second parameter is specified overlap and that's essentially saying let me use it in a different color that your chunks consecutive chunks should have some tokens um which are which exist in both for example because it was 200 the second chunk is not goin…\n\nCHAPTER 11 — Hello world graph coding example\n  [3:25:43] So which will be llm which will be start from l lm and the should continue function is the function which will be um the underlying function and this is a uh true false statement and this is the edge the set entry point all of this we've covered many times so…\n  [3:27:49] In 2024, the S&amp;P 500 delivered a total return of this with a 23% increase late 1990s and all of that stuff uh magnificent 7 and has given us the uh respective uh citations as well.\n  [3:28:13] Well, the reason I prom I asked it for this is because that's exactly what uh over here it stated the benchmark roughly at 25% 23%.\n  [3:28:24] Uh remember this late 1990s part that's exactly what this is saying here as well and this was correctly defined in the first document.\n\nFRAMEWORKS:\n  · State Graph — A blueprint‑like component that manages nodes, edges, and the shared state, ensuring unified workflow execution.\n  · Runnable — A modular, executable unit that can be combined like Lego bricks to build complex AI workflow pipelines.\n\nTAKEAWAYS:\n  · Use TypedDicts to enforce data structures and reduce runtime errors in Langraph projects.\n  · Treat the state as a shared memory board that all nodes read from and write to, enabling coherent workflow data flow.\n  · Model each operation as a node and connect them with edges (or conditional edges) to define execution order.\n  · Leverage tool nodes to wrap external utilities like API calls, keeping the graph modular and testable.\n  · Start with the simple hello‑world graph to grasp syntax and data flow before building full AI agents.\n\nQUOTES:\n  \"Now it's important to note that it doesn't perform any operations itself but it serves as the designated starting position for the graph's execution.\" [15:12]\n  \"So now we need to do something very very important and it gets annoying sometimes but um it's really a key habit which I want you to form and it is dog strings.\" [27:45]\n  \"Now here's another important part which is quite a common mistake especially like I have done this many times.\" [44:24]\n  \"Again, if you remember a few subsections ago, I told you there are multiple ways to be able to initialize the start and the end point.\" [1:02:28]\n  \